From: Project Luma <builds@project-luma.local>
Subject: [PATCH] lumaShare: the window ring follows the window's own outline

The shared window's ring was a 7px translucent violet border with a fixed
16px outer radius around the frame. Its inner radius (9px) did not match
the window's 15px corners, so the ring and the window parted at every
corner, and the band ended in a hard edge: a half glow cut off at 7px.

The ring is now drawn from the window's own corner radius
(lumaWindowCorners.windowCornerRadius(): Luma's 15px for every application
window, maximized and tiled included, none for fullscreen, Android and
Mutter-framed X11 windows). Every outline outside a rounded rectangle is the
same rectangle grown by the distance, so the ring is a function of the
distance from the window alone: a 2.5px violet line from the window's edge
out, then a halo that falls smoothly to zero 11px beyond it, inside the
ring's actor. It is concentric with the window at every offset (radius plus
distance) and square where the window is square.

The ring is eight pieces around the frame, four corners and four edges,
children of the CaptureHidden actor so none reaches a stream. Their images
depend only on the radius and the display's resolution (drawn at the
monitor's scale, so the line is crisp at 100%, 150% and 200%) and are shared
between rings; moving or resizing a window moves and stretches the pieces
and draws nothing.

diff --git a/data/theme/luma-share.css b/data/theme/luma-share.css
